**Locked.**

**Eveline — Head Weaver**  
Not only the silent council presence over the wing. She is the one who can actually work the source. Skill, not just title. The other Weavers answer to her craft as much as to her authority.

**The divide**

**Source chamber** (Eveline only, or those she alone permits)  
Aurora bound to the Seat. Hair splayed into the first loom. The true origin of the binding thread. No ordinary Weaver enters here. They are not told what it is.

**The collection halls** (the working Weavers)  
Thousands of strands emerge from the walls as if the stone itself were exhaling them. Each one looks like a ripple in the air — a heat-haze filament, barely visible, slightly resistant to the hand. No one here calls it hair. No one has reason to. They gather the ripples, twist them into stable cords, and carry those cords to the long chambers where the city-tapestry is woven into foundation and grid.

**What the rank-and-file believe**  
They are maintaining a necessary civic mystery. The walls produce the raw material; their craft makes it load-bearing; the city holds. Questions about the source are not encouraged and, after a while, stop occurring. The work is exacting, quiet, and framed as duty.

**What Eveline knows**  
The ripples are Aurora. The tapestry is continuous extraction. The city’s unity is not infrastructure with a strange feedstock — it is a person, still present, still feeding the loom.

The two layers never meet in conversation. Only in the material that passes through the walls.